WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.125
Threat hunting depends on available, trustworthy telemetry.

2
00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:10.740
A hunter can only reason from evidence that exists, is accessible, and can be interpreted correctly.

3
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:22.958
The best hypothesis will fail if the required data was never collected, is retained for too short a period, or lacks the fields needed to identify users, hosts, commands, or destinations.

4
00:00:22.958 --> 00:00:28.484
This is why telemetry review is part of the hunt, not a separate administrative task.

5
00:00:28.484 --> 00:00:41.948
Common data sources include endpoint process events, authentication logs, DNS logs, proxy and firewall logs, EDR telemetry, cloud control-plane logs, identity-provider logs, and email security logs.

6
00:00:41.948 --> 00:00:51.365
Endpoint process events help answer questions about parent-child process chains, command lines, file execution, and persistence behavior.

7
00:00:51.365 --> 00:01:00.003
Authentication logs help answer questions about account use, source locations, device changes, and failed or successful access.

8
00:01:00.003 --> 00:01:05.451
DNS and proxy logs help answer questions about domains and outbound destinations.

9
00:01:05.451 --> 00:01:13.934
Cloud control-plane logs show administrative actions such as role changes, key creation, storage access, or unusual API calls.

10
00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:23.117
Data quality matters as much as data presence. Missing fields can make a useful query impossible. Clock drift can make a timeline look wrong.

11
00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:30.900
Inconsistent hostnames can split one asset into several identities. NAT can cause many systems to share one source IP.

12
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:39.928
Short log retention can hide the start of an activity chain. Noisy event volume can bury relevant events under routine software behavior.

13
00:01:39.928 --> 00:01:48.877
A hunt should account for these issues instead of pretending the data is perfect. Trust also has layers. An endpoint agent may be offline.

14
00:01:48.877 --> 00:01:55.415
A cloud log may be delayed. A firewall log may show connection metadata without application detail.

15
00:01:55.415 --> 00:02:00.707
An identity log may show a successful sign-in but not what the user did afterward.

16
00:02:00.707 --> 00:02:05.843
Hunters should understand what each source can prove and what it cannot prove.

17
00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:15.105
A proxy log may show that a system connected to a domain, but it may not prove which process made the request unless endpoint data is correlated.

18
00:02:15.105 --> 00:02:17.751
Telemetry gaps are valid hunt findings.

19
00:02:17.751 --> 00:02:28.568
If the hunt cannot answer a question because DNS logs are missing from a network segment or cloud administrative logs are not retained long enough, document that gap.

20
00:02:28.568 --> 00:02:35.728
That outcome can drive logging improvements, retention changes, parsing fixes, or new detection coverage.

21
00:02:35.728 --> 00:02:49.503
A beginner threat hunter should build a habit of asking three telemetry questions before querying: what source should show this behavior, what fields do I need, and what limitations could weaken my conclusion?

22
00:02:49.503 --> 00:02:55.262
That habit keeps the investigation grounded in evidence and prevents overconfidence.

23
00:02:55.262 --> 00:03:00.010
Telemetry review should also include access and privacy expectations.

24
00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:09.427
Hunters should use approved data sources, follow retention and handling rules, and avoid collecting more information than the hunt requires.

25
00:03:09.427 --> 00:03:14.329
Good hunting respects both security needs and responsible data handling.

26
00:03:14.329 --> 00:03:22.112
The point is to answer the defensive question with enough evidence, not to expose unrelated user or business activity.
